TI  - The Differences Between Direct Alignment Algorithms are a Blur
AU  - Alexey Gorbatovski
AU  - Boris Shaposhnikov
AU  - Viacheslav Sinii
AU  - Alexey Malakhov
AU  - Daniil Gavrilov
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2026)
AB  - Direct Alignment Algorithms (DAAs) simplify LLM alignment by directly optimizing policies, bypassing reward modeling and RL. While DAAs differ in their use of SFT (one-stage vs. two-stage) and the scalar score they optimize (likelihood vs. odds ratios), the key performance drivers remain underexplored. We present a systematic comparison and analyze a previously overlooked axis - the ranking objective (pairwise vs. pointwise). To isolate this factor, we propose a unified training framework across DAAs by (i) converting one-stage methods (ORPO, ASFT) into a two-stage pipeline with an explicit SFT phase and (ii) introducing a $\beta$ parameter that places all methods in the same hyperparameter space and improves the quality of odds-ratio DAAs (ORPO, ASFT). Under this setup, the ranking objective emerges as the primary determinant of alignment quality, whereas the particular scalar score (policy–reference ratio vs. odds ratio) is secondary. We corroborate this on instruction-following tasks and further confirm it on math-reasoning benchmarks across model scales. Evidence suggests that this stems from how these objectives interact with prompt-specific biases, supported both by strictly controlled experiments and by observations on real data. Our findings underscore the need for nuanced evaluations in DAA research to avoid oversimplified claims of superiority.
